    {
      "id": "claim_6b6aba8a022b41bb90b83b54f7baf676",
      "statement": "The two halves of the consents gate are checked with different rigour by setup: the Guards rule step compares the rule's full condition text and status_code against what TOS_VERSION/PP_VERSION derive, so a version bump shows up as outstanding work and the re-run rewrites the rule, while the permission step checks only that userCanPatchOwnConsents exists and therefore leaves an already-present permission's mergeRequest — including one still stamping older versions — untouched. That asymmetry is the quiet failure mode: the rule compares the new constants while acceptance is stamped with the old ones.",
      "evidence": [
        {
          "resource": "repo://ulabase.setup.consents.ts#L220-L249",
          "version": "repo-lines-v1:sha256:56499f2d0ad5eed28fb40c71ad06fe70a7a4e81e66811d8d67d473627ab1b050:"
        },
        {
          "resource": "repo://ulabase.setup.consents.ts#L282-L317",
          "version": "repo-lines-v1:sha256:61bb3204eef5b835755b0b6f6ddeb8cd77fc51636fd8e642903f24bd6671900f:"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "claim_b6f142a0a4874abb92e430ce058dadd8",
      "statement": "The guard rule reads latestConsents/tos and latestConsents/pp from the JWT rather than the database, so a missing entry in the auth feature's account-properties-claims makes the version comparison false for every token-authenticated user — including ones who just accepted — blocking them permanently while the condition looks reasonable. Setup adds both claims by set-union rather than replacement, and deliberately omits the growing consents history from the token because a JWT payload is readable base64.",
      "evidence": [
        {
          "resource": "repo://ulabase.setup.consents.ts#L163-L163",
          "version": "repo-lines-v1:sha256:4bb93358150cc115e30752b7ebea212679a6225d930d21c0222f812c6f849702:"
        },
        {
          "resource": "repo://ulabase.setup.consents.ts#L251-L275",
          "version": "repo-lines-v1:sha256:4bd3daaf8213666dd87649a2f91d22ecbb0d85c018edc188f2f61381241cf693:"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "claim_e2541d60ab2a4077a5edf1bd7bad3482",
      "statement": "A 451 is the consents Guards rule refusing an authenticated-but-unaccepted user, whereas a 403 on the acceptance PATCH means the ACL refused the request before any guard had a say — nothing authorises PATCH /users/{userId} out of the box — so a missing userCanPatchOwnConsents permission presents as a plain authorization failure rather than a consents problem, since a guard never gets a say on a request the ACL already refused.",
      "evidence": [
        {
          "resource": "repo://src/consents-signal.ts#L39-L49",
          "version": "repo-lines-v1:sha256:f8f41520e42e7d4386ac041d9a3b62e333f4a29846fc784b07c4a28ec6db0568:"
        },
        {
          "resource": "repo://ulabase.setup.consents.ts#L220-L231",
          "version": "repo-lines-v1:sha256:418269763765cf6354c656d6e8c9949669b6cce795b178e799a84f88f99657f5:"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "claim_b0d4970df70b45e389a0a9ecfbfd2329",
      "statement": "Feature-flag drift manifests as a 403 on a form the app still renders, because a server feature that is off answers unauthenticated callers with 403. There is no second list to reconcile by hand: ulabase.setup.ts imports the same environment.features the app imports and derives the service's toggles from it — registration and verification from emailRegistration, password-reset from passwordReset, invitations from teamInvitations, oauth from oauthLogin — so re-running setup is the whole repair.",
      "evidence": [
        {
          "resource": "repo://ulabase.setup.ts#L14-L64",
          "version": "repo-lines-v1:sha256:3e8a388b6830e11a76dafbde4e675b17df86bacd200e631d4ea0b0a7b3773837:"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "claim_df23d90e2f3044a29a929368e0dae388",
      "statement": "The origin-allowlist step installs the origin-allowlist feature if absent and adds APP_URL (default http://localhost:5173) to allowed-origins additively — never replacing existing origins, so a service reached from both localhost and a deployed app keeps both. When the app is served from somewhere new, setup must be re-run with the new APP_URL or the origin added under Service → Origin Allowlist, or every request fails with 'origin is not allowed'; the same step also keeps frontend-url and frontend-app-url pointing at the app so verification, reset and invitation email links are not dead ends.",
      "evidence": [
        {
          "resource": "repo://ulabase.setup.ts#L131-L157",
